<% layout("/layout", { title: "How it works", appNav: "full" }) %>
<%/* A visual guide built from the app's own pieces (the sort answers, the list names and icons),
     so it stays in step with the real screens. */%>
<a class="back" href="<%= it.chrome?.back.href ?? '/' %>">← <%= it.chrome?.back.label ?? 'All tasks' %></a>
<article class="help">
  <header class="help-hero">
    <h1>How Task Sloth works</h1>
    <p class="lead">Get things out of your head quickly, then put each one where it belongs, so the list you look at is only what you can actually do.</p>
    <ol class="overview">
      <li><a href="#jot"><span class="emoji" aria-hidden="true">✏️</span>Jot</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sort"><span class="emoji" aria-hidden="true">🗂️</span>Sort</a></li>
      <li><a href="#do"><span class="emoji" aria-hidden="true">✅</span>Do</a></li>
      <li><a href="#check-in"><span class="emoji" aria-hidden="true">🔄</span>Check in</a></li>
    </ol>
  </header>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li class="step" id="jot">
      <h2>Jot it down</h2>
      <p>Type anything into the box at the top and tap Add. You don't have to decide anything yet.</p>
      <div class="mock-add" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="mock-input">call the dentist</span><span class="mock-button">Add</span>
      </div>
      <p class="mock-result">→ 📥 It goes to your <strong>Inbox</strong>, which only you can see.</p>
      <p class="tip">💡 Add a detail, like <code>fri</code> or <code>@Target</code>, and it skips the Inbox and goes straight onto your list. Tap <strong>?</strong> next to the box for more.</p>
    </li>

    <li class="step" id="sort">
      <h2>Sort your inbox</h2>
      <p>When you have a minute, tap <strong>Sort my inbox</strong>. It shows one thing at a time and asks <em>“What is this?”</em></p>
      <ul class="choice-tiles">
        <% it.choices.forEach((c) => { %>
          <li><span class="tile-icon emoji" aria-hidden="true"><%= c.icon %></span><strong><%= c.label %></strong><span class="hint"><%= c.short %></span></li>
        <% }) %>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li class="step" id="do">
      <h2>Do things</h2>
      <p>Pick a list to see what's there. On a phone they're in the bar at the bottom of the screen.</p>
      <ul class="list-guide">
        <% [
          ["inbox", "Inbox", "What you've jotted down and not sorted yet"],
          ["mine", "Mine", "What's yours, soonest first"],
          ["all", "Everyone", "The whole household's list"],
        ].forEach(([icon, name, what]) => { %>
          <li><span class="guide-icon"><%~ include("/partials/nav-icon", { name: icon }) %></span><strong><%= name %></strong><span class="hint"><%= what %></span></li>
        <% }) %>
        <li class="has-more">
          <span class="guide-icon"><%~ include("/partials/nav-icon", { name: "more" }) %></span><strong>More</strong><span class="hint">Everything else:</span>
          <ul>
            <% it.nav.more.forEach((m) => { %>
              <li><strong><%= it.views[m.view] %></strong> <span class="hint">· <%= m.blurb %></span></li>
            <% }) %>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="tip">✏️ Tap the pencil on a task to rename it right there. Shortcuts work there too: renaming it to <code>call grandma sun @phone</code> sets the day and where. Tap its name to open it and see everything else.</p>
      <p class="tip">🙋 See something up for grabs? Tap <strong>Claim</strong> to take it.</p>
      <p class="tip">▶️ Doing something right now? Tap <strong>Start</strong> on it, and it goes to the top of your lists under “Working on now”, and everyone else sees “Sam's on it”, so nobody doubles up. It stops when you tick it off, tap <strong>Stop</strong>, or the day ends.</p>
      <p class="tip">🗂️ Like to see it all at once? Under the tabs, switch <strong>View as</strong> to <strong>Board</strong>: To do, Doing, Waiting on and Done today side by side. Drag a card to another column (on a phone, swipe between columns and tap <strong>Move to</strong>), and the task changes to match.</p>
      <p class="tip">🎯 Got a run of things in one place or project? Tap its chip, then <strong>Focus on these</strong> (or pick one under More → Focus). It shows one task at a time, with Done, Skip and Not today, and everyone sees what you're working through.</p>
      <p class="tip">🛒 Out and about? Tap a chip like <strong>@Target</strong> on any task to see everything you can do there.</p>
    </li>

    <li class="step" id="check-in">
      <h2>Check in now and then</h2>
      <p>Once in a while, look at <strong>Waiting on</strong> to see if you need to nudge anyone, and at <strong>Maybe later</strong> to see if something's ready. Tap <strong>Ready to do →</strong> to move it onto your list.</p>
      <p class="tip">⏳ Something that can't start until another task is done, like painting after patching the walls? Open it, tap <strong>Edit</strong>, and choose the other task under <strong>Blocked by another task</strong>. It stays on your list, marked ⏳, and is ready to go as soon as that task is ticked off.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <section class="help-card" id="shortcuts">
    <h2>Shortcuts you can type</h2>
    <p class="hint">You never need these, but they save taps. Type them anywhere in a task. Start with <code>@</code>, <code>#</code> or <code>+</code>, or a day like <code>tom</code>, and the add box suggests the rest: tap one to use it.</p>
    <%~ include("/partials/shortcuts", it.shortcuts) %>
  </section>

  <footer class="help-footer">
    <a class="button primary" href="/">Back to my tasks</a>
    <p class="hint">This way of working comes from a method called <em>Getting Things Done</em> by David Allen. You don't need to know it to use the app.</p>
  </footer>
</article>
